{"kind":"report","text":"Approved: delta re-review of the supported-host repair at exact pushed head fc63ec4037e32cf41a24ed22aff0413e3b8183b8, per request fda4ec31. All five requested repairs are present and each is proved by a mutation that the previous head survived.\n\nR1 resolution. cmd/gs/main.go introduces the package-private seam `var lookupIP = net.LookupIP`, and validateLoopbackListen refuses when resolution errors, returns no address, or returns any non-loopback address. TestValidateLoopbackListenRejectsMixedResolution stubs a host resolving to both 127.0.0.1 and 192.0.2.1 and requires refusal. Mutation: rewriting the loop to \"accept if any resolved address is loopback\" now FAILS that test (\"listener accepted a hostname with a non-loopback resolution\"). This is the exact mutation that survived the previous head.\n\nR2 browser credential recovery. The new ui/src/lib/lease.ts renewCredential clears the credential only when announce rejects with the resident's fixed message \"credential is not valid\" and a credential was actually held; every other error is rethrown, so session.ts sets live=false and credentialRef keeps its value. On clearing, the next 15s heartbeat announces with no credential and the resident mints a fresh lease. Two tests cover both branches. Mutations: never clearing FAILS \"a rejected browser credential is forgotten and the next heartbeat re-mints\"; clearing on any Error FAILS \"a transport failure does not discard a browser credential\". The wire string is pinned server-side by internal/service/server_test.go, so the coupling is guarded at both ends, and it matches the pattern cmd/gitseq-mcp already uses.\n\nR3 renew-time expiry with a controlled clock. Hub gains `now func() time.Time`, set to time.Now in the single constructor NewWithSigningKey (New delegates to it, so no path leaves it nil), and every time.Now() call inside the hub now reads h.now(). Hub.Expire is gone entirely rather than merely unexported; tests drive the clock with setHubNow. TestCredentialsAreResidentScopedAndExpire advances that clock and calls RenewSessionIdentity with no explicit expiry call. Mutation: deleting the h.expire(h.now()) sweep from RenewSessionIdentity now FAILS with \"expired credential renewed\". This mutation also survived the previous head.\n\nR4 documentation. docs/reference/gs/serve.md states that every --listen hostname including localhost is resolved through the host resolver, that resolution must succeed, return at least one address, and return only loopback addresses, and that a host failing to resolve or resolving to both loopback and non-loopback is refused. The refusal table now carries both messages separately, for a literal non-loopback address and for a hostname that fails to resolve or resolves to anything non-loopback.\n\nR5 simplifications. validCredential's shape check runs before the presence lookup in both RenewSessionIdentity and RevokeSession. Session.id is renamed Session.credential throughout ui/src and ui/test, so no secret sits behind an id-named field; App.tsx and Thread.tsx read session.credential. No exported Expire remains anywhere.\n\nArchitecture. Affected layers are 3 (nexus and live runtime, internal/nexus) and 7 (CLI, MCP, connectors and UI: cmd/gs, cmd/gitseq-mcp, internal/service, ui, docs). internal/app is untouched: its tree hash is byte-identical to origin/main, as are internal/kernel, internal/gitstore, internal/workroom and internal/custody. The contracts of layers 3 and 7 are PRESERVED by this delta. The supported-host posture already published in docs/reference/architecture.md at the previous head 8b427acf is unchanged here; this head only strengthens the hostname resolution the page already describes and adds a client-side recovery path. The docs artifact stands at this head, so the contract page and the behaviour it names are anchored together.\n\nSecurity. Trust and authority boundaries: the resident stays a trusted local custodian for one operator account, requiring an explicit per-invocation --acknowledge-trusted-processes. Nothing login-like grew; the no-partial-auth ruling is preserved. Untrusted inputs: --listen is operator input and is now more tightly bounded, since a hostname must resolve, resolve to something, and resolve only to loopback. The lookupIP seam is a package-level var in package main, unexported and written only by a test that restores it with t.Cleanup, so no external code can retarget it. The remaining resolve-then-bind TOCTOU on hostnames is inherent and unreachable inside this posture: an actor who can move the operator's resolver is already inside the boundary, and the default listener is a literal loopback address that never resolves. Credentials and secrets: the credential is still minted by the resident, held only in tab memory, never persisted (asserted by test), and never echoed; a mutation that put the attempted credential into a refusal FAILS TestResidentCredentialNeverAppearsOutsideCreationResponse. Clearing on the resident's refusal shortens rather than lengthens the window a dead credential is held. Bounds and failure modes: re-minting is bounded by the 30s lease TTL and the 15s heartbeat, and LiveSessionsForActor is a report with no cap for a re-mint loop to exhaust; a transport outage now degrades to live=false with the credential intact instead of orphaning it. Spot-check mutations on the prior seams all fail as they should: bypassing the acknowledgement lets an unacknowledged resident publish and serve; making validateLoopbackListen a no-op fails six cases including the profiler guard.\n\nSimplification. One residual item, not blocking. validCredential is now provably redundant: neutralizing its shape check leaves the whole suite green, because the `!exists` presence lookup immediately after it refuses every value the shape check would refuse, with the identical message, and validCredential has no other caller. The request offered \"shape check before lookup OR removed\", and this head took the permitted first option, so the condition of satisfaction is met; recording it here so a later pass can delete the function and its two call sites, keeping only the direct shape test in credential_test.go if the minting format is worth pinning. Everything else in the delta is minimal: lease.ts is fifteen lines with one exported function, and the clock injection replaced an exported mutator with a field.\n\nGates at this exact head, in a clean detached worktree: go test -count=1 ./... EXIT 0; go test -race -count=1 ./... EXIT 0; npm ci \u0026\u0026 npm test in ui/ EXIT 0 (61 pass, 0 fail); make ui-check EXIT 0, so the committed embed matches a fresh build; go build ./... clean; go vet ./... clean; gofmt -l reports nothing; git status --porcelain --untracked-files=normal empty; HEAD equals fc63ec4037e32cf41a24ed22aff0413e3b8183b8. The docset gates run inside go test ./... and passed with -count=1, so the documentation edits were not served from cache.\n\nNote for whoever merges: all six artifacts are live and none describes a superseded world, but each carries succession_unrecorded with two or three live predecessors at the same path. Retiring those predecessors is the merge step's work under AGENTS.md step 5, and this verdict co-signs all six paths so the receipt can reach them.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:bac269bed916481d6ad354f67da7a18f5c2edad2","head":"fc63ec4037e32cf41a24ed22aff0413e3b8183b8","stale":"true","staleness":"artifact, promise, request stale; retired bases: git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:0c15ae61a553e3f65a036087b157fb47010f3793, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:22e522fe58eef152ef3ad253c86a11a51db03532, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:23c336b4c36d39576422c1f2ec6171cb1810679f, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:29439846217f934baf0582e15ab6814bf6ca37c8 and 20 more","verdict":"approved"